window.unit2Data = {"topicNumber": 2, "topicName": "Ecosystems and Global Biodiversity", "sections": [{"sectionTitle": "Ecosystems and biodiversity", "dotpoints": [{"number": 12, "text": "The nature and complexity of ecosystem functioning and global biodiversity, including energy flows and nutrient cycles; dynamic equilibrium and feedback loops; relationships between natural systems", "notes": ["An ecosystem is a community of living organisms (biotic components) interacting with the non-living physical environment (abiotic components); it has inputs (solar radiation, precipitation, nutrients), processes (photosynthesis, respiration, nutrient cycling, decomposition) and outputs (biomass, heat, water vapour).", "Structure: producers (autotrophs), consumers (herbivores/carnivores/omnivores) and decomposers/detritivores (fungi, bacteria, invertebrates) that recycle matter.", "'Ecosystem functioning' is the collective biological, geochemical and physical processes that maintain an environment's integrity, stability and productivity and provide ecosystem services.", "Biodiversity (genetic, species, ecosystem levels) enhances stability, productivity and resilience; loss undermines ecosystem services and can trigger collapse.", "Energy flows: solar energy drives photosynthesis; Gross Primary Productivity (GPP) = total energy captured; Net Primary Productivity (NPP) = GPP minus plant respiration, i.e. energy available to consumers. Productivity varies by biome (rainforests/coral reefs high; deserts/tundra low).", "Food chains show direct pathways; food webs show real interconnected feeding relationships \u2014 a change in one population (e.g. apex predator decline) cascades through the web.", "Trophic levels run producers -> primary consumers -> secondary consumers -> tertiary consumers -> decomposers. The 10% rule: only ~10% of energy passes to the next trophic level, most lost as heat via respiration, so biomass/numbers shrink at higher levels.", "Nutrient cycles move elements (carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium) between stores (biomass, soil, litter) via flows (decomposition, weathering, uptake, leaching, rainfall), sustaining plant growth and soil fertility.", "Tropical rainforest nutrient cycling: large biomass store, small soil store (rapid uptake, intense rainfall, fast decomposition, shallow roots) \u2014 efficient but fragile; deforestation removes litter input, accelerates leaching and erosion, reduces fertility.", "Dynamic equilibrium: ecosystems stay balanced as inputs/outputs fluctuate within limits, existing when changes to a system are matched by its ability to cope with/adapt to that change; resilience supports equilibrium, vulnerability indicates how easily it is disrupted.", "Feedback loops: positive feedback amplifies change (e.g. Arctic sea ice melt lowering albedo, causing more warming and more melt); negative feedback counteracts change and stabilises the system (e.g. predator-prey regulation).", "Tipping points are thresholds beyond which rapid, often irreversible change occurs (e.g. coral reef collapse, savannisation of rainforest, Arctic sea-ice loss); deforestation can trigger a positive feedback loop of nutrient-poor soil, reduced plant growth, drought, fire and more deforestation.", "Relationships between natural systems: ecosystem interdependence (plants need light/water/nutrients, animals need plants/other animals, microbes recycle nutrients); feedbacks link atmosphere-hydrosphere-biosphere-lithosphere; symbiosis (mutualism, parasitism, commensalism) underpins nutrient cycling and species survival (e.g. mycorrhizae and trees); global heat budget and wind/ocean currents redistribute heat and connect regions so local actions can have global consequences."], "caseStudies": [{"name": "Positive feedback example \u2014 Arctic sea ice melt", "facts": ["Rising temperatures melt sea ice, reducing ice cover and lowering albedo (reflectivity).", "Lower albedo means more solar heat is absorbed rather than reflected, accelerating further melting \u2014 an amplifying (positive) feedback loop."]}, {"name": "Negative feedback example \u2014 predator-prey regulation", "facts": ["An increase in prey provides more food for predators, so predator numbers rise.", "Rising predator numbers reduce prey numbers, which in turn causes predator numbers to decline, stabilising the system around a balanced population."]}], "data": ["10% rule: only ~10% of energy transfers to the next trophic level; ~90% is lost as heat via respiration.", "NPP = GPP minus plant respiration."], "flashcards": [{"term": "Gross Primary Productivity (GPP)", "definition": "The total energy/biomass captured by producers through photosynthesis in an ecosystem."}, {"term": "Net Primary Productivity (NPP)", "definition": "GPP minus the energy used by plants for respiration; the energy actually available to consumers."}, {"term": "10% rule", "definition": "Only around 10% of energy is transferred from one trophic level to the next, with the rest lost mainly as heat through respiration."}, {"term": "Dynamic equilibrium", "definition": "A balanced state in an ecosystem where continual change occurs, but inputs and outputs stay within limits the system can adapt to, keeping it viable."}, {"term": "Positive vs negative feedback", "definition": "Positive feedback amplifies a change (e.g. ice-albedo melt), driving a system further from its original state; negative feedback counteracts change and helps restore stability (e.g. predator-prey cycles)."}, {"term": "Tipping point", "definition": "A threshold beyond which an ecosystem undergoes rapid, often irreversible change to a new state, e.g. coral reef collapse or rainforest savannisation."}, {"term": "Mutualism / parasitism / commensalism", "definition": "Types of symbiosis: mutualism benefits both species, parasitism benefits one and harms the other, commensalism benefits one while the other is unaffected (e.g. mycorrhizae and tree roots)."}]}, {"number": 13, "text": "The value of ecosystems and biodiversity", "notes": ["Biodiversity underpins ecological balance and planetary processes, contributes to human wellbeing, and its loss can cause ecosystem collapse affecting food security and health; the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) is a global initiative to protect it.", "Ecosystem services are commonly grouped under the 'SCaRP' framework: Supporting, Cultural, Regulating and Provisioning services (note: the source guide's own acronym breakdown has a formatting gap/blank bullet, but the four categories it describes are these).", "Supporting services underpin all other services (nutrient cycling, soil formation, primary production) and are vital for agriculture/food security but often economically overlooked.", "Cultural services are non-material benefits (recreation, spirituality, aesthetics, education); ecotourism and protected areas support appreciation, conservation and community wellbeing, and Indigenous perspectives emphasise cultural significance and stewardship.", "Regulating services maintain environmental balance (climate regulation, water purification, disease control, pollination); coastal ecosystems reduce storm impacts/erosion and wetlands filter water/reduce flood risk, but climate change threatens these services.", "Provisioning services are material resources essential for survival (food, freshwater, timber, fibres, medicine); overexploitation can deplete resources and destabilise ecosystems.", "Utility value: ecosystems support global economic functions (forestry, fisheries, pastoralism, tourism) and human adaptability; species loss removes potential food/medicine/material sources.", "Intrinsic value: ecosystems have inherent worth beyond economics, providing emotional, spiritual and aesthetic benefits and ensuring resources for future generations; ethical perspectives emphasise responsibility to protect nature."], "caseStudies": [{"name": "Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD)", "facts": ["A global initiative/agreement aimed at protecting biodiversity.", "Reflects biodiversity's rise to prominence as a sustainability priority in the late 20th century."]}], "data": [], "flashcards": [{"term": "Ecosystem services (SCaRP)", "definition": "The benefits ecosystems provide to people, grouped as Supporting, Cultural, Regulating and Provisioning services."}, {"term": "Supporting services", "definition": "Services that underpin all other ecosystem services, such as nutrient cycling, soil formation and primary production."}, {"term": "Provisioning services", "definition": "Material resources ecosystems supply that are essential for human survival, such as food, freshwater, timber and medicine."}, {"term": "Utility value", "definition": "The economic and practical usefulness of ecosystems to humans, e.g. through forestry, fisheries, pastoralism and tourism."}, {"term": "Intrinsic value", "definition": "The inherent worth of ecosystems and biodiversity independent of their economic usefulness to humans, including emotional, spiritual and ethical value."}, {"term": "Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD)", "definition": "A global agreement/initiative aimed at protecting the world's biodiversity."}]}, {"number": 14, "text": "The relationship between ecological and human stresses, and the vulnerability and resilience of ecosystems, including ecological integrity and biocapacity", "notes": ["All ecosystems function in dynamic equilibrium \u2014 continual balanced change from interactions between natural elements \u2014 but because elements are interdependent, changes beyond equilibrium limits can prevent a return to the original state.", "Stresses can be ecological (natural) or human-induced, and each can be catastrophic (sudden, e.g. drought, flood, fire, deforestation, mining) or gradual (e.g. natural climatic change, species adaptation, anthropogenic climate change, soil compaction, groundwater depletion).", "Vulnerability = how susceptible an ecosystem is to disturbance, shaped by biodiversity, nutrient levels, climate sensitivity and human pressure; resilience = an ecosystem's ability to recover after disturbance.", "Resilience concepts: elasticity (rate of recovery of a property after disturbance), amplitude (threshold of strain beyond which the system can't return to its original state), and malleability (the difference between final recovery level and pre-stress level \u2014 greater difference means less resilience).", "Causes of ecosystem vulnerability \u2014 the 'BELL' framework: Biodiversity (greater diversity improves resilience), Extent (larger ecosystems cope better; small/fragmented ones like Minnamurra are more vulnerable), Location (latitude/altitude/climate affect stress exposure), Linkages (strong interconnections reduce vulnerability, weak linkages increase risk).", "Ecological integrity measures whether an ecosystem's structure, composition and function resemble a natural, undisturbed state; high integrity exists without major human alteration, and depends on healthy interaction of the biosphere, lithosphere, atmosphere and hydrosphere.", "Biocapacity is an ecosystem's ability to regenerate resources and provide services, measured in global hectares (gha); sustainability requires consumption at or below biocapacity.", "Ecological footprint measures human demand on ecosystems (also in gha); a biocapacity deficit occurs when footprint exceeds regenerative ability, causing 'ecological overshoot'."], "caseStudies": [{"name": "Coral reefs", "facts": ["Highly vulnerable ecosystems due to temperature sensitivity and human impacts."]}, {"name": "Tropical rainforests", "facts": ["Demonstrate resilience through high biodiversity and strong nutrient cycling."]}, {"name": "Minnamurra (small/fragmented ecosystem)", "facts": ["Used as an example under the 'Extent' factor of the BELL framework: small, fragmented ecosystems are more vulnerable to disturbance than large, continuous ones."]}, {"name": "Great Barrier Reef", "facts": ["Its ecological integrity is monitored through coral cover and species diversity."]}], "data": ["In 2016, global biocapacity was approximately 1.6 gha per person.", "Around 85% of the global population lives in countries operating in ecological (biocapacity) deficit.", "Global biocapacity declined from ~3 gha/person (1961) to <2 gha/person (2018), while ecological footprint rose from ~2.2 to ~2.7 gha/person, crossing biocapacity around the early 1970s."], "flashcards": [{"term": "Vulnerability", "definition": "How susceptible an ecosystem is to disturbance, influenced by biodiversity, nutrient levels, climate sensitivity and human pressure."}, {"term": "Resilience", "definition": "An ecosystem's capacity to absorb disturbance and recover its function afterward."}, {"term": "Elasticity / amplitude / malleability", "definition": "Elasticity is the rate of recovery after disturbance; amplitude is the strain threshold beyond which recovery to the original state fails; malleability is the gap between the final recovered state and the pre-stress state."}, {"term": "BELL framework", "definition": "A framework for causes of ecosystem vulnerability: Biodiversity, Extent, Location, Linkages."}, {"term": "Ecological integrity", "definition": "How closely an ecosystem's structure, composition and function resemble its natural, undisturbed state."}, {"term": "Biocapacity", "definition": "An ecosystem's capacity to regenerate resources and absorb waste, measured in global hectares (gha)."}, {"term": "Biocapacity deficit / ecological overshoot", "definition": "Occurs when a population's ecological footprint exceeds the biocapacity available to it, meaning resources are used faster than they can regenerate."}]}, {"number": 15, "text": "The global state of ecosystems and biodiversity, including current and future trends and reasons for the trends; shifting baselines and tipping points", "notes": ["Biodiversity has been declining for roughly two centuries, accelerated since the Industrial Revolution; the Living Planet Index (LPI) shows a downward trend in vertebrate populations globally, weakening ecosystem services, with tropical regions showing the steepest declines.", "Reasons for current trends \u2014 human activities: industrialisation, deforestation, pollution, climate change, urbanisation/land-use change and invasive species introduced via trade; post-WWII growth accelerated extraction and degradation.", "Reasons for current trends \u2014 economic/sociopolitical factors: short-term economic goals often outweigh sustainability; resource-extraction economies degrade ecosystems; instability in low/middle-income countries hinders conservation (e.g. Amazon deforestation driven by agriculture and logging); neoliberal policies have shaped outcomes.", "Reasons for current trends \u2014 climate change: alters habitats, increases extinction risk, disrupts ecological interactions; coral reefs are among the most vulnerable ecosystems (e.g. Great Barrier Reef bleaching); IPCC reports stress urgency of mitigation.", "Future trends: recovery is possible with decisive action before 2030; 'nature positive' frameworks aim to restore/protect ecosystems; reforestation and marine protected areas show measurable success; Indigenous knowledge supports restoration.", "The 'Nature Positive by 2030' model (Locke et al. 2021) projects biodiversity indicators bottoming out around 2030 (zero net loss from 2020) then rising toward full recovery by 2050 if action is taken.", "COP15 commitments: the '30x30' target \u2014 protecting 30% of land and restoring 30% of degraded ecosystems by 2030 \u2014 aims to build a global network of protected areas, building on the CBD; funding and governance are central to meeting targets.", "Shifting baselines: loss of knowledge about historical ecosystem conditions leads to lowered expectations/tolerance of degradation and poor management decisions based on inaccurate baselines (e.g. overfished populations now seen as 'normal').", "Tipping points are critical thresholds where small changes cause irreversible ecosystem shifts (e.g. mass coral bleaching, Amazon savannah transition); the 'ball in a basin' model shows a resilient system (Basin A) that can be pushed by stress into a different state (Basin B) or further into another state (Basin C)."], "caseStudies": [{"name": "Living Planet Index (LPI) \u2014 WWF/ZSL 2022 report", "facts": ["Tracks the average change in relative abundance of 31,821 populations representing 5,230 species monitored globally, 1970-2018.", "Found an average decline of approximately 68-69%, with a 95% statistical confidence range of 63%-75%."]}, {"name": "Historical species recoveries", "facts": ["The American bison and the California condor are cited as examples of successful historical species recovery."]}, {"name": "COP15 / 30x30 target and EU Natura 2000", "facts": ["COP15 saw global leaders commit to protecting 30% of land and restoring 30% of degraded ecosystems by 2030 ('30x30').", "EU Natura 2000 is cited as an example of large-scale conservation success building a protected-area network."]}], "data": ["~68-69% average decline in monitored vertebrate population abundance from 1970-2018 (WWF/ZSL Living Planet Report 2022; 95% CI 63%-75%), based on 31,821 populations of 5,230 species.", "Average % of key biodiversity areas covered by protected areas (1970-2018): developed economies rose from ~10% to ~68%; world average rose to ~45%; developing/least developed economies rose to ~33-34%."], "flashcards": [{"term": "Living Planet Index (LPI)", "definition": "A composite index that tracks trends in the abundance of vertebrate populations globally, used as a barometer of biodiversity health."}, {"term": "Nature positive", "definition": "A framework/goal aiming to halt and reverse biodiversity loss, restoring and protecting ecosystems so nature is measurably better off by a set date."}, {"term": "COP15 / 30x30 target", "definition": "A global commitment made at COP15 to protect 30% of land and restore 30% of degraded ecosystems by 2030."}, {"term": "Shifting baseline", "definition": "The gradual change in what is perceived as 'normal' environmental conditions, caused by loss of knowledge of historical ecosystem states, leading to lowered expectations of degradation."}, {"term": "Tipping point", "definition": "A critical threshold beyond which a small additional change causes rapid, often irreversible shifts in an ecosystem's state."}, {"term": "'Ball in a basin' model", "definition": "A model of tipping points where a system (ball) sits in a stable basin (normal state) but can be pushed by stress over a threshold into a different basin (a new, potentially less desirable, state)."}]}, {"number": 16, "text": "Strategies for the sustainable management of ecosystems at a range of scales, including at least one successful conservation program", "notes": ["Ecosystems are managed at a range of scales: global (international agreements like the CBD and Paris Agreement provide a conservation framework), national (legislation such as the EPBC Act enforces commitments), regional (states/regions add frameworks outside national law), local (local governments run community programs) and individual (personal actions and volunteering).", "Global strategy example: the Paris Agreement aims to limit global warming to well below 2\u00b0C (efforts toward 1.5\u00b0C), representing collective global action on climate impacts to ecosystems.", "Regional strategy example: the Amazon Cooperation Treaty Organization (ACTO) promotes sustainable development and conservation across the Amazon Basin, balancing development with protection in a highly biodiverse region.", "National strategy example: Australia's National Parks network protects diverse ecosystems and native flora/fauna, providing wildlife habitat and raising environmental awareness.", "Local strategy example: urban green spaces (parks, green roofs, urban forests) enhance biodiversity, mitigate urban heat, improve air quality and provide habitat.", "Individual strategy example: reducing one's ecological footprint through recycling, water/energy conservation and gardening with native plants.", "Successful conservation program case study: 'Saving the Last Tigers of Asia' (Thailand) \u2014 combines habitat protection, anti-poaching, community involvement, international cooperation, research/monitoring and awareness campaigns, and has scaled from one national park to a national/regional model."], "caseStudies": [{"name": "Saving the Last Tigers of Asia (Thailand)", "facts": ["Asia has lost over 95% of tiger habitat, and fewer than 4,000 tigers remain in the wild; tigers are extinct in China, Cambodia, Laos and Vietnam, with significant remaining populations only in India and Nepal.", "Threats include hunting/poaching syndicates, illegal logging, habitat fragmentation (reducing breeding and genetic diversity), overhunting of prey, and illegal wildlife trade driven by demand for tiger parts in traditional medicine and luxury goods.", "Key protected areas: the Eastern Forest Complex (Thap Lan National Park) and the Western Forest Complex (Thung Yai-Huai Kha Khaeng National Parks).", "Strategy combines habitat protection, anti-poaching patrols/laws, community involvement, international cooperation, research/monitoring, and awareness campaigns.", "Over 150 cellular trailcams were deployed in Thap Lan to stop poaching, a success that led to nationwide adoption; 'Global Park Defense' now provides surveillance, SMART patrolling, rapid response and legal support.", "Outcomes: poaching sharply reduced in Thap Lan, tigers returning with new cubs observed, and the strategy is now used as a model for tiger conservation across Asia."]}], "data": ["Asia has lost over 95% of tiger habitat.", "Fewer than 4,000 tigers remain in the wild.", "Over 150 cellular trailcams were deployed in Thap Lan National Park to combat poaching."], "flashcards": [{"term": "Paris Agreement", "definition": "A global climate agreement aiming to limit global warming to well below 2\u00b0C, with efforts to limit it to 1.5\u00b0C, addressing climate impacts on ecosystems."}, {"term": "Amazon Cooperation Treaty Organization (ACTO)", "definition": "A regional body promoting sustainable development and conservation across the Amazon Basin."}, {"term": "EPBC Act", "definition": "Australia's national environmental legislation that translates international biodiversity commitments into enforceable domestic action."}, {"term": "SMART patrolling / Global Park Defense", "definition": "A technology-supported anti-poaching approach used in Thailand's tiger conservation program, providing surveillance, rapid response and legal support, scaled nationally after success at Thap Lan National Park."}, {"term": "Thap Lan National Park", "definition": "Part of Thailand's Eastern Forest Complex where trailcam deployment sharply reduced poaching and tiger numbers began recovering, becoming a national model."}]}, {"number": 17, "text": "The role played by Indigenous Peoples in contemporary management practices", "notes": ["Indigenous cultures often view themselves as custodians of the land with deep responsibility toward nature, seeing land as sacred and to be protected for future generations, emphasising harmony rather than exploitation.", "Ecosystem management principles include gathering food/shelter while maintaining ecological balance, self-sufficiency (taking only what is needed), and holistic approaches considering interconnected species and ecosystems.", "Spiritual connections to land shape management: seasonal calendars guide planting, harvesting and cultural rituals, and tools/materials reflect traditional ecological knowledge (TEK).", "Knowledge is transmitted through laws, codes of conduct, rituals, customs, stories and teachings, preserving ancestral ecological wisdom and cultural identity across generations.", "Indigenous nations established territories and governance systems long before 1788, with approximately 600-700 distinct nations, each with unique laws and cultural practices; language groups contain multiple clans responsible for particular lands, and totems represent spiritual/ecological connections to land.", "Contemporary practice bridges traditional knowledge and Western science, supporting environmental, cultural, social and economic outcomes."], "caseStudies": [{"name": "Indigenous Land and Sea Rangers", "facts": ["Rangers integrate traditional ecological knowledge with Western science, acting as a bridge between cultural knowledge and contemporary management.", "Activities include fire management using traditional burning techniques, feral animal and invasive species control, and community engagement/inter-generational knowledge sharing.", "Their work supports environmental, cultural, social and economic outcomes."]}, {"name": "Regeneration of Jewell Wetlands", "facts": ["Regeneration involved consultation with Indigenous communities to ensure culturally appropriate practices, including the use of fire stick farming.", "Controlled/ecological burning stimulated soil seed banks and supported vegetation regeneration, demonstrating success of blending Indigenous knowledge with modern conservation."]}], "data": ["Approximately 600-700 distinct Indigenous nations existed in Australia prior to 1788, each with unique laws and cultural practices."], "flashcards": [{"term": "Stewardship / custodianship", "definition": "The Indigenous view of holding deep responsibility for caring for and protecting land as sacred, for the benefit of future generations."}, {"term": "Totems", "definition": "Symbols representing a clan or individual's spiritual and ecological connection to particular land, species or places."}, {"term": "Fire stick farming", "definition": "Traditional Indigenous controlled/cultural burning practice used to manage vegetation, promote biodiversity and stimulate regeneration (e.g. of soil seed banks)."}, {"term": "Traditional Ecological Knowledge (TEK)", "definition": "Indigenous knowledge systems, built and passed down over generations, that inform sustainable ecosystem management."}, {"term": "Indigenous Land and Sea Rangers", "definition": "A program in which Indigenous rangers combine traditional ecological knowledge with Western science to manage fire, control invasive/feral species and engage communities."}]}]}, {"sectionTitle": "Investigation of TWO ecosystems", "dotpoints": [{"number": 18, "text": "The characteristics of the ecosystem, including its spatial pattern and the nature of its biodiversity", "notes": [], "caseStudies": [{"name": "Borneo Tropical Rainforest", "facts": ["World's third-largest island, ~743,330 sq km, shared by Indonesia, Malaysia and Brunei, located on the equator.", "Forest types (spatial pattern): montane forests (900-3,300m), heath/Kerangas forests (poor acidic soils), mangrove forests (estuaries/coast), peat swamp forests (lowland, high carbon storage), and lowland dipterocarp forests (most biodiverse, trees over 45m tall).", "Dipterocarp family: ~700 species, name means 'two-winged fruit' (Greek), largest species reach 80-93m and form the structural backbone of the forest; some species stay in the understory their whole lives.", "Climate (Kota Kinabalu climograph): equatorial, hot and humid year-round, 2,000-4,000mm rainfall annually, 27-30\u00b0C average temperature, humidity often above 80%, dry season May-Oct and wet season Nov-Apr; temperature decreases with altitude.", "Flora: over 15,000 plant species including orchids, pitcher plants and giant dipterocarps. Fauna: orangutans, pygmy elephants, clouded leopards, hornbills, plus high invertebrate diversity; high endemism due to isolation, recognised as a global biodiversity hotspot.", "Named food-web species include clouded leopard, tiger, reticulated python, elephant, fruit bat, orangutan, spotted deer and Sunda bearded pig as consumers, and grasses, rambutan, palm shoot, durian, jackfruit and fig as producers."], "notes": ["The two investigated ecosystems are the Borneo Tropical Rainforest and the Great Southern Reef (GSR) kelp forest off southern Australia \u2014 a terrestrial vs marine comparison, both recognised as globally significant, highly biodiverse systems with high endemism.", "Borneo: the world's third-largest island (~743,330 sq km), shared by Indonesia, Malaysia and Brunei, straddling the equator, with an equatorial climate (hot, humid year-round, 2,000-4,000mm rainfall, 27-30\u00b0C average, humidity often above 80%, two monsoon seasons).", "Borneo's forest types form a spatial pattern: montane forests (900-3,300m, shorter adapted trees), heath forests/Kerangas (poor acidic soils), mangrove forests (estuaries/coastal zones), peat swamp forests (lowland, large carbon stores) and lowland dipterocarp forests (most biodiverse, tall evergreen trees over 45m).", "Dipterocarps are the structural backbone of Borneo's forests: ~700 species, ranging from shrubs to giant canopy emergents reaching 80-93m; they provide fruit/pollen/nectar/habitat and are often climax species, but regeneration is limited by local seed source availability.", "Borneo vegetation is layered: emergent (60m+ dipterocarps), canopy (dense foliage blocking light), understory (shade-tolerant shrubs/saplings) and forest floor (low light, nutrient-rich decomposing material)."], "data": ["Borneo: ~743,330 sq km land area; over 15,000 plant species; dipterocarps up to 80-93m tall."]}, {"name": "Great Southern Reef (kelp forest)", "facts": ["Located along the continental shelf of Southern Australia, dominated by Golden Kelp (Ecklonia radiata) as the key foundation species; ~80% of species are endemic in some regions.", "Spatial pattern: extends ~8,000 km from northern NSW to Kalbarri, WA, covering ~71,000 km\u00b2 of continental shelf, at depths of 30-60m below the low-tide mark.", "Biodiversity: 731 fish species, 350+ invertebrate species, 1,500+ seaweed species, plus marine mammals (dolphins, seals), cephalopods (squid, octopus) and crustaceans (crabs, lobsters).", "Ecological dynamics: whale migration contributes to nutrient cycling and energy flow; nutrient supply is influenced by the East Australian Current (EAC) and its interaction with Bass Strait waters; a stable continental shelf, including basalt flows, provides the geological foundation for kelp attachment."], "notes": ["The two investigated ecosystems are the Borneo Tropical Rainforest and the Great Southern Reef (GSR) kelp forest off southern Australia \u2014 a terrestrial vs marine comparison, both recognised as globally significant, highly biodiverse systems with high endemism.", "GSR: located along the continental shelf of Southern Australia, dominated by the foundation species Golden Kelp (Ecklonia radiata); extends ~8,000 km from northern NSW to Kalbarri, WA, covering ~71,000 km\u00b2 at depths of 30-60m (sub-tidal zone).", "GSR ecological dynamics: whale migration contributes to nutrient cycling/energy flow, the East Australian Current (EAC) influences nutrient supply, and a stable continental shelf (including basalt flows) supports kelp attachment; kelp forests also contribute to carbon sequestration."], "data": ["GSR: ~8,000 km spatial extent; ~71,000 km\u00b2 of continental shelf; 731 fish species; 350+ invertebrate species; 1,500+ seaweed species; depth range 30-60m."]}], "data": [], "flashcards": [{"term": "Dipterocarp", "definition": "The dominant tree family of Borneo's lowland rainforest (~700 species), forming the emergent canopy and structural backbone of the forest; name means 'two-winged fruit'."}, {"term": "Kerangas (heath forest)", "definition": "A Bornean forest type on acidic, nutrient-poor soils, supporting specially adapted flora such as pitcher plants."}, {"term": "Golden Kelp (Ecklonia radiata)", "definition": "The foundation species of the Great Southern Reef, forming extensive underwater kelp forests along southern Australia's continental shelf."}, {"term": "Endemism", "definition": "The occurrence of species restricted to a particular geographic area; both Borneo and the GSR show high endemism (GSR ~80% in some regions) due to isolation and unique evolutionary conditions."}, {"term": "East Australian Current (EAC)", "definition": "A warm ocean current that influences nutrient supply and temperature along Australia's east coast, shaping conditions for the Great Southern Reef."}]}, {"number": 19, "text": "The dynamics of ecosystem functioning, including vulnerability, resilience and ecological disturbance", "notes": [], "caseStudies": [{"name": "Borneo Tropical Rainforest", "facts": ["Nutrient cycle: decomposers -> soil nutrients -> plant growth -> primary consumers, with rapid uptake by vegetation leaving little stored in soil, producing thin, leached, ferralitic (iron-rich, red) soils.", "Disturbance types: floods (topsoil removal, drowned seedlings, spread of invasives/pathogens) and landslides (triggered by monsoon rain or deforestation, disrupting water and nutrient flow).", "Pioneer/resilient species: Macaranga tree (rapid coloniser, deep roots, mycorrhizal associations), Nepenthes pitcher plants (carnivory for nitrogen/phosphorus in poor soils), Nipa palm (tidal flooding tolerance), and fast-growing ferns/grasses stabilising exposed soil.", "Secondary forest regeneration depends on nearby seed sources, wind/animal dispersal of dipterocarp seeds, and uncompacted soil for native regrowth."], "notes": ["Borneo nutrient cycling: most nutrients are stored in biomass rather than soil because warm, moist conditions support fast decomposers and rapid uptake by vegetation; soils are thin, nutrient-poor, heavily leached and often iron-rich/red (ferralitic).", "Borneo linkages: complex food webs (orangutans, hornbills, dipterocarps, fungi, invertebrates) depend on tight interdependencies \u2014 e.g. dipterocarps rely on specific animals for seed dispersal, mycorrhizal fungi help roots absorb scarce nutrients, and decomposers (ants, termites, beetles, worms) aerate soil and recycle organic matter; habitat fragmentation disrupts these interdependencies and raises vulnerability.", "Borneo disturbances: floods (common in lowland areas, remove topsoil, drown seedlings, spread invasive species/pathogens) and landslides (triggered by monsoon rain or deforestation, remove vegetation, disrupt water/nutrient flow, can block rivers). Disturbances can be acute (short-term) or chronic (long-term).", "Borneo resilience/pioneer species: Macaranga trees rapidly colonise disturbed sites; pitcher plants (Nepenthes spp.) thrive in poor soils via carnivory; Nipa palm survives tidal flooding via floating seeds; fast-growing ferns/grasses (e.g. Dicranopteris linearis, Imperata cylindrica) stabilise exposed soil; secondary forest regeneration depends on nearby seed sources and uncompacted soil."], "data": ["Borneo's rainforest nutrient store is concentrated in biomass rather than soil, due to rapid uptake and intense equatorial rainfall/leaching."]}, {"name": "Great Southern Reef (kelp forest)", "facts": ["Primary production can reach up to 65 tonnes of biomass per hectare annually, supporting fisheries such as those in California and Alaska.", "Kelp forms complex three-dimensional habitat structures that provide breeding/feeding grounds and increase biodiversity, while also influencing local hydrodynamics and nutrient flow.", "Vulnerability factors: warming waters (tropicalisation), sedimentation (reduces light, smothers kelp) and runoff-driven algal blooms.", "Resilience factors: sexual and asexual reproduction for recolonisation, flexible stipes that withstand wave/current stress, and marine protected areas; Southern California kelp declined due to urchin grazing then recovered after sea otters were reintroduced to control urchin numbers."], "notes": ["GSR ecosystem functioning: primary production via photosynthesis (up to 65 tonnes of biomass per hectare annually) underpins marine food webs; kelp forms complex 3D habitat structures for shelter, breeding and feeding; kelp absorbs and releases nutrients, reducing eutrophication and supporting adjacent ecosystems (e.g. seagrass beds); kelp forests also sequester carbon.", "GSR food web dynamics: kelp is the primary food source for herbivores which in turn support predators; sea otters regulate urchin populations to prevent kelp overgrazing.", "GSR vulnerability: threats include warming waters (enabling tropicalisation \u2014 warm-water species spreading into temperate zones), sedimentation (reduces light, smothers kelp) and runoff-driven algal blooms.", "GSR resilience: kelp reproduces both sexually and asexually to recolonise disturbed areas, has flexible stipes that withstand waves/currents, and benefits from marine protected areas and genetic diversity; example \u2014 otter reintroduction in Southern California restored kelp forests after urchin-driven decline."], "data": ["GSR kelp production can reach up to 65 tonnes of biomass per hectare annually."]}], "data": [], "flashcards": [{"term": "Ferralitic soil", "definition": "An iron-rich, red, nutrient-poor tropical soil typical of rainforests like Borneo's, formed by intense leaching of nutrients under heavy rainfall."}, {"term": "Macaranga tree", "definition": "A rapid-colonising pioneer tree species in Borneo that stabilises disturbed sites (e.g. logging sites, landslides) with deep roots and mycorrhizal associations."}, {"term": "Tropicalisation", "definition": "The poleward spread of warm-water species into temperate ecosystems (like the GSR) as ocean temperatures rise, disrupting existing food webs."}, {"term": "Sea otters and urchin regulation", "definition": "Sea otters are a keystone predator in kelp forest ecosystems (e.g. Southern California) that control sea urchin populations, preventing overgrazing of kelp; their removal or reintroduction directly affects kelp forest health."}, {"term": "Ecological disturbance (acute vs chronic)", "definition": "An event that disrupts ecosystem structure/function; acute disturbances are short-term (e.g. a single flood or landslide) while chronic disturbances are long-term, with impact depending on the ecosystem's resilience."}]}, {"number": 20, "text": "Human-induced modifications to the ecosystem", "notes": [], "caseStudies": [{"name": "Borneo Tropical Rainforest", "facts": ["Large-scale deforestation driven by timber/agricultural land demand and export-driven policy caused increased runoff, flooding, soil erosion and reduced carbon sequestration.", "Commercial logging of high-value hardwoods (e.g. meranti), often illegal due to weak enforcement, dries the forest floor via canopy gaps and fragments habitat.", "Palm oil plantation expansion in Indonesia and Malaysia replaces rainforest with monocultures, causing biodiversity loss, burning-related CO2/pollution and chemical runoff.", "Coal/gold/mineral mining causes severe landscape degradation and mercury/sediment contamination of rivers.", "Slash-and-burn fire use on drained, flammable peatlands causes large-scale regional haze events and major carbon emissions."], "notes": ["Borneo was once almost entirely forested; rapid deforestation in the 1980s-1990s (shown in the source guide's 1950-2020 map sequence) has left only a fraction of original rainforest today.", "Borneo driver 1 \u2014 large-scale deforestation: caused by global demand for timber/agricultural land, export-driven government policy and settlement/infrastructure expansion; consequences include increased runoff/flooding/erosion, reduced carbon sequestration, and habitat loss for keystone species.", "Borneo driver 2 \u2014 commercial logging: high-value hardwoods (e.g. meranti) harvested for international markets with weak enforcement enabling illegal logging; consequences include canopy gaps drying the forest floor (raising fire risk), habitat fragmentation and reduced climate resilience.", "Borneo driver 3 \u2014 palm oil plantation expansion: driven by global demand (food, cosmetics, biofuels) and cheap land/incentives in Indonesia and Malaysia; consequences include biodiversity loss from monocultures, CO2/particulate emissions from land-clearing burns, and soil degradation/chemical runoff.", "Borneo driver 4 \u2014 mining (coal, gold, minerals): driven by mineral/energy demand, government licensing and poverty-driven artisanal mining; consequences include severe landscape degradation, mercury/sediment river contamination and long-term pollution from abandoned mines.", "Borneo driver 5 \u2014 fire use and accidental burning: caused by slash-and-burn clearing and flammable drained peatlands, with fires escaping plantations in dry seasons; consequences include regional haze events, major carbon emissions from peatland destruction and reduced regrowth potential from soil nutrient loss."], "data": ["Borneo forest cover mapped across 1950/1985/2000/2005/2010/2020 shows progressive, large-scale forest loss."]}, {"name": "Great Southern Reef (kelp forest)", "facts": ["Overfishing (e.g. the collapse of North Atlantic cod stocks, and overfishing of bluefin tuna) causes cascading ecological and economic effects; Maximum Sustainable Yield (MSY) is used to guide global fisheries management.", "Climate change drives coral bleaching, ocean acidification and kelp forest decline through thermal stress and tropicalisation (warm-water species invading temperate waters).", "Urbanisation, coastal development, agricultural runoff and sedimentation destroy habitat and cause eutrophication, reducing light and smothering kelp.", "Case study \u2014 Jervis Bay: 1980 scallop dredging caused local scallop extinction; mussel farming led to invasive mussel dominance; microplastics and PFAS threaten marine organisms; ocean warming/heatwaves are shifting species distributions south, with tropical arrivals often dying in winter, and acidification threatens kelp and associated species."], "notes": ["GSR pressure 1 \u2014 overfishing: reduces fish populations/diversity and disrupts food webs; removing top predators can increase smaller fish populations and overgrazing on algae; Maximum Sustainable Yield (MSY) is used to guide fisheries management globally.", "GSR pressure 2 \u2014 climate change: rising sea temperatures cause coral bleaching and kelp decline; ocean acidification threatens shellfish, corals and other calcifying organisms; tropicalisation sees warm-water species invade and disrupt temperate systems, with dependent fish species declining where kelp is lost.", "GSR pressure 3 \u2014 habitat destruction/coastal change: urbanisation and coastal development destroy kelp forests and reefs; agricultural runoff/pollution cause eutrophication; sedimentation smothers kelp and reduces light; coastal structures alter wave energy/sediment movement, making MPAs essential for restoration."], "data": ["Jervis Bay: 1980 scallop dredging caused local scallop extinction."]}], "data": [], "flashcards": [{"term": "Maximum Sustainable Yield (MSY)", "definition": "The largest catch that can be taken from a fish stock over the long term without depleting it; used globally to guide fisheries management."}, {"term": "Palm oil plantation expansion (Borneo)", "definition": "Conversion of Bornean rainforest to oil palm monocultures driven by global demand for palm oil, causing biodiversity loss, burning-related emissions and soil/water pollution."}, {"term": "Jervis Bay scallop dredging", "definition": "1980 scallop dredging in Jervis Bay caused the local extinction of scallops, illustrating human-induced modification of the GSR ecosystem."}, {"term": "Ocean acidification", "definition": "The decrease in ocean pH from absorbing atmospheric CO2, which impairs calcification in organisms such as shellfish and corals."}, {"term": "North Atlantic cod stock collapse", "definition": "A well-known example of overfishing causing the collapse of a major fish stock and triggering cascading ecological effects through the food web."}]}, {"number": 21, "text": "Responses and strategies, including for maintaining ecosystem functioning and actions for sustainability", "notes": [], "caseStudies": [{"name": "Borneo Tropical Rainforest", "facts": ["Launched in 2007 through a joint declaration by Brunei, Indonesia and Malaysia; a government-led, WWF-supported initiative.", "Four focus areas: conserving forests/biodiversity; sustainable land use and green economic development (including mandatory sustainable palm oil certification, MSPO, in Malaysia); enhancing communities' quality of life (including support for Indigenous Dayak communities and ecotourism); and reducing spatial inequality via transboundary collaboration.", "Reported to benefit both biodiversity and around 11 million people living in the region."], "notes": ["Borneo \u2014 WWF Heart of Borneo (HoB) Project: launched 2007 via a joint declaration by Brunei, Indonesia and Malaysia; a government-led, NGO-supported (WWF) initiative aiming to conserve biodiversity and support dependent communities through protected areas, sustainable forest management and sustainable land use.", "HoB pillar 1 \u2014 conserving forests/biodiversity: expands protected-area networks across borders, maintains forest connectivity, implements sustainable forest management to prevent conversion of natural forest, and recognises the region as a biodiversity hotspot.", "HoB pillar 2 \u2014 sustainable land use/green economy: promotes sustainable agricultural planning, mandatory sustainable palm oil certification (MSPO) in Malaysia, green economy initiatives and a Green Business Network supporting conservation-friendly practices.", "HoB pillar 3 \u2014 enhancing communities' quality of life: community-based conservation and poverty alleviation, ecotourism development, support for Indigenous Dayak communities and protection of culturally significant forests, and education on sustainable resource management.", "HoB pillar 4 \u2014 reducing spatial inequality: sustainable rural development in forest-edge communities, conservation districts empowering local governance, infrastructure/livelihood improvements via ecotourism/sustainable agriculture, and transboundary collaboration; the initiative is said to benefit both biodiversity and 11 million people in the region."], "data": ["WWF Heart of Borneo Project (launched 2007) is reported to benefit biodiversity and approximately 11 million people in the region."]}, {"name": "Great Southern Reef (kelp forest)", "facts": ["Marine Protected Areas such as the Great Barrier Reef Marine Park conserve habitat by limiting harmful activity, requiring monitoring, enforcement and community involvement.", "Restoration techniques include replanting kelp, removing invasive species and restoring sediment dynamics, with successful biodiversity increases seen in restoration examples such as California.", "Sustainable practice measures include catch limits, selective fishing gear, reduced bycatch, pollution reduction and integration of Traditional Ecological Knowledge (TEK).", "Management also includes regulating fishing quotas, controlling invasive species like long-spined sea urchins, protecting key species (kelp, abalone, rock lobsters), and biodiversity surveys/monitoring to inform decisions, alongside co-management frameworks that include Indigenous custodians."], "notes": ["GSR \u2014 Marine Protected Areas (MPAs): designated regions limiting harmful activity to conserve habitats (e.g. the Great Barrier Reef Marine Park), requiring monitoring, enforcement, adaptive management and community involvement.", "GSR \u2014 restoration projects: rehabilitate degraded kelp forests via replanting kelp, removing invasive species and restoring sediment dynamics, with adaptive management and community collaboration (e.g. successful biodiversity increases seen in California).", "GSR \u2014 sustainable practices: catch limits, selective gear and reduced bycatch prevent overexploitation; pollution reduction and education support habitat health; integrating Traditional Ecological Knowledge (TEK) enhances conservation.", "GSR \u2014 broader management: regulating fishing quotas, controlling invasive species (e.g. long-spined sea urchins), protecting key species (kelp, abalone, rock lobsters), running biodiversity surveys/monitoring, restoring seagrass/coral/sponge gardens, and integrating First Nations leadership/TEK into co-management."], "data": []}], "data": [], "flashcards": [{"term": "WWF Heart of Borneo (HoB) Project", "definition": "A 2007 joint conservation initiative by Brunei, Indonesia and Malaysia (government-led, WWF-supported) that protects forest biodiversity, promotes sustainable land use and supports local communities, benefiting an estimated 11 million people."}, {"term": "Mandatory Sustainable Palm Oil (MSPO) certification", "definition": "A Malaysian certification scheme requiring palm oil production to meet sustainability standards, used as part of the Heart of Borneo's green economy strategy."}, {"term": "Marine Protected Area (MPA)", "definition": "A designated marine area that restricts harmful human activities (e.g. fishing, development) to conserve biodiversity and habitats, such as the Great Barrier Reef Marine Park."}, {"term": "Kelp restoration techniques", "definition": "Methods used to rehabilitate degraded kelp forests, including replanting kelp, removing invasive species (e.g. sea urchins) and restoring sediment dynamics."}, {"term": "Traditional Ecological Knowledge (TEK) in GSR management", "definition": "The integration of First Nations knowledge and leadership into co-management frameworks for the Great Southern Reef, supporting sustainable fishing and aquaculture."}]}, {"number": 22, "text": "Differences in ecosystem management, compared with at least one other location, due to economic, political and sociocultural factors", "notes": [], "caseStudies": [{"name": "Borneo Tropical Rainforest", "facts": ["The source guide names Daintree Tropical Rainforest as the intended comparison location but provides no developed content on it under this dot point \u2014 a genuine gap in the source material, not an extraction error."], "notes": ["Source guide's coverage of this dot point for Borneo is incomplete \u2014 flagged for follow-up. The guide's contents page names 'Daintree Tropical Rainforest' as the intended comparison location, but no actual content was written under that heading (it moves straight to the Great Southern Reef case study), and the heading is annotated 'After trials', suggesting it was meant to be completed post-Term 3 trials and never was. This needs supplementing with independent research on Daintree Rainforest management (e.g. comparing Australian/Queensland legislative and Indigenous co-management approaches against Indonesian/Malaysian/Bruneian approaches under the Heart of Borneo initiative)."], "data": []}, {"name": "Great Southern Reef (kelp forest)", "facts": ["GASF is managed primarily by South Africa's Department of Forestry, Fisheries and the Environment (DFFE) under the Marine Living Resources Act (1998), within the African Union's Sustainable Forest Management Strategy (2020-2030), aligned with SDG 15 and Agenda 2063.", "Economic support comes via the Green Fund (public-private partnerships, constrained by national budgets) and monitoring technology like Kelp Watch (satellite imagery plus machine learning).", "Sociocultural drivers include the Sea Change Project (Craig Foster's 'My Octopus Teacher') and the '1001 Seaforest Species' initiative, blending science with Indigenous knowledge and storytelling.", "Compared with GSR, GASF management relies more on monitoring technology and cultural storytelling within a continental policy framework, whereas GSR relies more on formal MPAs, restoration programs and university research consortia within Australia's national park/MPA system."], "notes": ["GSR is compared with the Great African Sea Forest (GASF) off South Africa and Namibia, another extensive kelp forest system with rich marine biodiversity and cultural/ecological significance.", "GASF political framework: led by national governments, primarily South Africa's Department of Forestry, Fisheries and the Environment (DFFE); the Marine Living Resources Act (1998) regulates kelp harvesting across subsistence, recreational and commercial sectors; the African Union's Sustainable Forest Management Strategy (2020-2030) provides a continental framework aligned with SDG 15 and Agenda 2063.", "GASF economic factors: the Green Fund provides targeted financial support for biodiversity/ecosystem-service projects, encouraging public-private partnerships but constrained by national budgets; technology investment (e.g. Kelp Watch, using satellite imagery and machine learning) focuses on monitoring rather than restoration.", "GASF sociocultural factors: conservation is driven by cultural storytelling and emotional connection, exemplified by the Sea Change Project (led by filmmaker Craig Foster, whose documentary 'My Octopus Teacher' brought global attention to the GASF) and initiatives like '1001 Seaforest Species', which blends marine biology with Indigenous knowledge, underwater photography and oral history.", "Contrast: GSR management is more institutionally/scientifically driven (MPAs, formal restoration programs, university-led research consortia), while GASF leans more on monitoring technology plus cultural storytelling/documentary media within a continental policy framework rather than a single-country park/MPA network; both are led by national government departments/legislation, but funding models differ (Australian public agency funding vs South Africa's externally-reliant Green Fund grant model)."], "data": []}], "data": [], "flashcards": [{"term": "Great African Sea Forest (GASF)", "definition": "An extensive kelp forest ecosystem along the coasts of South Africa and Namibia, used as a comparison location for the Great Southern Reef in studying differences in ecosystem management."}, {"term": "Marine Living Resources Act (1998)", "definition": "South African legislation regulating kelp harvesting across subsistence, recreational and commercial sectors to prevent overexploitation of the Great African Sea Forest."}, {"term": "Sea Change Project / 'My Octopus Teacher'", "definition": "A South African conservation initiative led by filmmaker Craig Foster; his documentary 'My Octopus Teacher' used cultural storytelling to bring global attention to the Great African Sea Forest."}, {"term": "Kelp Watch", "definition": "A monitoring technology used in the Great African Sea Forest that applies satellite imagery and machine learning to track kelp forest dynamics over time."}, {"term": "Borneo/Daintree comparison gap", "definition": "The source revision guide names Daintree Rainforest as Borneo's intended management comparison but never develops any content on it \u2014 a content gap flagged for independent research."}]}, {"number": 23, "text": "The role of contemporary research and innovation in the sustainable management of the ecosystem", "notes": [], "caseStudies": [{"name": "Borneo Tropical Rainforest", "facts": ["The source guide provides only an empty heading for this dot point with no developed content \u2014 a genuine gap in the source material, not an extraction error."], "notes": ["Source guide's coverage of this dot point for Borneo is incomplete \u2014 flagged for follow-up. The heading '2.12 The role of contemporary research and innovation...' appears on page 30 of the guide with no content written beneath it before the document moves on to the Great Southern Reef case study. This needs independent supplementing, e.g. research on trailcam/AI monitoring networks, satellite deforestation tracking (such as Global Forest Watch), orangutan genetic/population research, or WWF Heart of Borneo scientific monitoring programs."], "data": []}, {"name": "Great Southern Reef (kelp forest)", "facts": ["Purple sea urchin overgrazing (worsened by nutrient pollution from wastewater and shifting nutrient conditions across the 1980s nutrient surge and the Millennium drought) produced barren rocky reefs, prompting restoration efforts.", "The Queenscliff Marine Science Centre cultivates over 6 million Golden Kelp spores on twine and gravel for replanting, in collaboration with The Nature Conservancy, the University of Melbourne, Deakin University and Parks Victoria.", "Victoria's first Golden Kelp Seed Bank preserves genetic diversity by collecting reproductive material from multiple Bay locations to support long-term, genetically diverse restoration.", "The Green Gravel Technique seeds juvenile kelp (in a microscopic, lab-cultivable life stage) onto gravel for scalable, community-accessible deployment into degraded habitats, and allows selection of thermally tolerant genotypes to future-proof kelp forests against climate change."], "notes": ["GSR \u2014 Australian researchers lead global efforts in marine habitat restoration along the Great Southern Reef; restoration (resource-intensive) is used when natural recovery is insufficient, with active projects restoring seaweeds, seagrasses and oyster reefs.", "Case study focus \u2014 Port Phillip Bay: overgrazing by purple sea urchins severely reduced kelp/seaweed habitats, worsened by nutrient pollution from wastewater treatment; 1980s nutrient surges increased weedy seaweed (more urchin food), then the Millennium drought reduced nutrients and urchins consumed kelp directly, producing barren rocky reefs that prompted restoration.", "Kelp replanting initiative: the Queenscliff Marine Science Centre grows juvenile Golden Kelp for replanting, with over 6 million spores cultivated on twine and gravel, aiming at scalable restoration technology; managing purple urchin numbers is essential for regrowth; collaborators include The Nature Conservancy, University of Melbourne, Deakin University and Parks Victoria.", "Golden Kelp Seed Bank: Victoria's first Golden Kelp seed bank preserves genetic diversity by collecting reproductive material from multiple Bay locations, supporting long-term restoration with diverse genotypes, with plans to expand seed collection along Victoria's coastline.", "Green Gravel Technique: integrates restoration ecology with natural kelp reproduction by seeding juvenile seaweeds onto gravel (using a microscopic kelp life stage that is easy to cultivate in a lab) for distribution into degraded habitats; the technique is scalable, customisable and community-accessible, and enables selection of thermally tolerant kelp genotypes to future-proof habitats against climate change, including for sessile species unable to migrate."], "data": ["Over 6 million Golden Kelp spores cultivated on twine and gravel at the Queenscliff Marine Science Centre for Port Phillip Bay restoration."]}], "data": [], "flashcards": [{"term": "Green Gravel Technique", "definition": "A kelp restoration method that seeds juvenile kelp (grown from a microscopic life stage) onto gravel in the lab, then distributes it into degraded reef habitats; it is scalable and allows selection of thermally tolerant kelp genotypes."}, {"term": "Golden Kelp Seed Bank", "definition": "Victoria's first kelp seed bank, preserving genetic diversity by storing reproductive material collected from multiple locations in Port Phillip Bay to support long-term restoration."}, {"term": "Port Phillip Bay kelp collapse", "definition": "Kelp and seaweed habitat loss in Port Phillip Bay caused by purple sea urchin overgrazing, compounded by nutrient pollution shifts (1980s nutrient surge followed by the Millennium drought), producing barren rocky reefs."}, {"term": "Queenscliff Marine Science Centre", "definition": "A facility growing juvenile Golden Kelp (over 6 million spores cultivated on twine/gravel) for replanting in Port Phillip Bay, in collaboration with organisations including The Nature Conservancy, University of Melbourne, Deakin University and Parks Victoria."}, {"term": "Borneo research/innovation content gap", "definition": "The source revision guide's heading for this dot point under Borneo has no content written beneath it, requiring independent research (e.g. trailcam/AI monitoring, Global Forest Watch, orangutan genetic research) to supplement."}]}]}]};
window.topic2WrittenData = [{"id": "hsc2025_q18b", "source": "2025 HSC", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [15], "marks": 4, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain ONE trend in global biodiversity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (Question 18(b)):\n4 marks - Explains ONE trend in global biodiversity\n3 marks - Describes ONE trend in global biodiversity\n2 marks - Outlines ONE trend in global biodiversity\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: One trend in global biodiversity is the rapid decline in wildlife populations, with a dramatic decrease especially in the last 50 years. This trend is driven by habitat loss, climate change, pollution, overexploitation, and invasive species. Habitat destruction, particularly from agriculture and urbanisation, fragments ecosystems, making it difficult for species to survive resulting in population decline. Climate change makes these effects worse by altering habitats and food sources."}, {"id": "hsc2025_q23", "source": "2025 HSC", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [12], "marks": 20, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain the nature and complexity of ecosystem functioning.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (Question 23, band marking):\n17-20 marks - Demonstrates comprehensive knowledge and understanding of the nature and complexity of ecosystem functioning; Provides a detailed explanation of the nature and complexity of ecosystem functioning; Integrates relevant examples, geographical information and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate to support the response; Presents a sustained, logical and cohesive response using appropriate geographical ideas, terms and concepts\n13-16 marks - Demonstrates well-developed knowledge and understanding of the nature and complexity of ecosystem functioning; Provides an explanation of the nature and complexity of ecosystem functioning; Refers to relevant examples, geographical information and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate to support the response; Presents a logical response using appropriate geographical terminology and concepts\n9-12 marks - Demonstrates sound knowledge and understanding of the nature and/or complexity of ecosystem functioning; Describes the nature and/or complexity of ecosystem functioning; Refers to examples, geographical information and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate; Presents a structured response using appropriate geographical concepts\n5-8 marks - Demonstrates some knowledge and understanding of ecosystem functioning; May refer to examples, geographical information and/or the Stimulus Booklet; Uses some geographical terminology\n1-4 marks - Demonstrates a basic understanding of ecosystem functioning\n\nAnswers could include:\nNature of ecosystem functioning may include: abiotic and biotic interactions; spatial patterns; energy flows and nutrient cycles; dynamic equilibrium and feedback loops; relationships between natural systems.\nAspects of ecosystem complexity may include: adaptation and change such as succession; biodiversity and interdependence; differing combinations of natural systems/spheres resulting in unique and/or fragile diversity; fluctuations in functioning resulting in altered or different vulnerability and resilience."}, {"id": "sample_17a", "source": "Sample Exam", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [17], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe the role that can be played by Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems.", "markingGuideline": "Official marking guideline (top band, 4 marks): Provides characteristics and features of the role that can be played by Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems. (3 marks: provides SOME characteristics and features; 2 marks: demonstrates a basic understanding; 1 mark: provides some relevant information.) Official sample answer: Many Indigenous cultures have strong connections with, and a deep understanding of, the land and how to manage and care for it, which stem from knowledge passed down for thousands of years. Indigenous Peoples can play an important role in the contemporary management of ecosystems through consultation and joint decision-making. Indigenous Peoples' knowledges and approaches can be incorporated into contemporary management strategies to enhance sustainability. Strategies such as closed seasons, sustained burning and having protected species are examples of Indigenous Peoples' customary management approaches which have been adapted into contemporary ecosystem management."}, {"id": "sample_22", "source": "Sample Exam", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [22], "marks": 20, "directive": "Account for", "question": "Account for the differences in the management of ONE type of ecosystem at TWO different locations.", "markingGuideline": "Official marking guideline (top band, 17-20 marks): Demonstrates comprehensive knowledge and understanding of the differences in the management of ONE type of ecosystem at TWO different locations; provides highly-developed reasons for the differences; integrates relevant examples, geographical information and sources where appropriate; presents a sustained, logical and cohesive response using appropriate geographical information, ideas, terms and concepts. (Lower bands: 13-16 well-developed; 9-12 sound with some reasons; 5-8 some knowledge, may refer to examples; 1-4 basic understanding.) Official 'answers could include' guidance: Reference to one type of ecosystem at two different locations that can be in Australia or overseas, for example Great Barrier Reef and New Caledonia Coral Reef, or Florida Everglades wetland ecosystem and Okavango Delta wetland ecosystem. Details of management strategies at each location such as: national park designation, landuse zoning, legislation, reforestation, fishing quotas, landuse agreements with Indigenous Peoples, closed seasons, wildlife breeding programs. Reasons for differences in ecosystem management could include: economic factors, politics, sociocultural factors (such as level of development, cultures, Indigenous Peoples' practices, ideology, nature of the economy, land ownership, business interests and government policy). Responses may refer to differences in complexity, enforcement, monitoring, compliance, and the success of management strategies at each location due to the influences identified."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q18a", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [16], "marks": 2, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe ONE conservation program that has successfully managed an ecosystem.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (2 marks): Provides the features and characteristics of ONE conservation program that has successfully managed an ecosystem - 2 marks; Sketches in general terms, some features and/or characteristics of ONE conservation program that has successfully managed an ecosystem - 1 mark. Answers could include: The GBRMPA conserves the Great Barrier Reef by zoning to limit harmful activities, promoting sustainable tourism, monitoring reef health, addressing coral bleaching, and improving water quality to preserve biodiversity. The Badu ITW in Sydney Olympic Park is conserved through habitat restoration, invasive species control, and tidal flow management. Sustainable public access via boardwalks balances education with ecosystem protection, enhancing biodiversity and ecological health."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q18b", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [17], "marks": 4, "directive": "Examine", "question": "Examine the role played by Indigenous peoples in contemporary ecosystem management practices.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (4 marks): Provides a detailed inquiry into the role played by Indigenous Peoples in more than one contemporary ecosystem management practice, and provides relevant examples to support the response - 4 marks; Provides an inquiry into the role played by Indigenous Peoples in contemporary ecosystem management practices, may provide a relevant example - 3 marks; Provides general information about the role played by Indigenous Peoples in ecosystem management practices, may only provide information on one management practice - 2 marks; Provides relevant information - 1 mark. Answers could include: Indigenous Peoples play a key role in ecosystem management by combining traditional knowledge with modern practices. For example, the Firestick Alliance in Australia uses controlled burns to reduce bushfire risks and support biodiversity. Similarly, Kaitiakitanga in New Zealand sees Maori communities manage the Whanganui River as a living entity, ensuring environmental sustainability and cultural connection. These approaches demonstrate how Indigenous knowledge enhances conservation efforts."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q18c", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [13], "marks": 5, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain the value of ecosystems and biodiversity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (5 marks): Demonstrated deep knowledge and understanding of the value of ecosystems and biodiversity, supports answer with a range of relevant examples - 5 marks; Provides a general understanding of the value of ecosystems and biodiversity, may provide a relevant example - 4 marks; Describes some of the values of ecosystems and biodiversity - 3 marks; Provides some basic understanding of the value of ecosystems and biodiversity - 2 marks; Provides some relevant information - 1 mark. Answers could include: Ecosystems and biodiversity are essential for maintaining ecological balance, supporting human well-being, and providing vital services such as clean air, water, and food. For example, the Amazon Rainforest is a global carbon sink, regulating climate and supporting countless species. Similarly, coral reefs, such as the Great Barrier Reef, protect coastlines and provide habitat for marine life. Biodiversity also supports agriculture, with species such as bees playing a crucial role in pollination. Protecting ecosystems ensures the survival of these services, highlighting their intrinsic and economic value."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q21a", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [18], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Select ONE ecosystem from Australia OR ONE ecosystem from outside Australia. Using the selected ecosystem, answer Question 21.\n(a) Describe the characteristics of the ecosystem, including its spatial pattern and the nature of its biodiversity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (4 marks): Clearly identifies ONE ecosystem from Australia OR ONE ecosystem from outside of Australia, detailed description of the characteristics of the ecosystem, including its spatial pattern and the nature of its biodiversity - 4 marks; Clearly identifies ONE ecosystem, describes the characteristics of the ecosystem, including its spatial pattern and the nature of its biodiversity - 3 marks; Identifies ONE ecosystem, outlines features and some characteristics of the ecosystem - 2 marks; Provides some relevant information - 1 mark. Sample Answers - Australian Ecosystems: Coral Reef-Great Barrier Reef; Intertidal Wetlands-Homebush Bay, Towra Point; Alpine Ecosystem-Kosciuszko National Park. Outside Australia: Wetlands-Florida Everglades; Fiordland-New Zealand; Okavango Delta. Sample paragraph: The Coral reef ecosystem of the Great Barrier Reef is the largest coral ecosystem in the world. Located off the east coast of Queensland it extends for 2300km between 8 degrees S and 24 degrees S and covers an area of 344,000km2. The coral reef ecosystem of the Great Barrier Reef is one of the most biologically diverse ecosystems in the world, with over 400 species of coral and 1625 species of fish found within the reef."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q21b", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [20], "marks": 6, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Select ONE ecosystem from Australia OR ONE ecosystem from outside Australia. Using the selected ecosystem, answer Question 21.\n(b) Explain how ONE human-induced modification influences this ecosystem.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (6 marks): Clearly identifies ONE ecosystem from Australia OR ONE ecosystem from outside of Australia, detailed explanation of how ONE human induced modification influences the ecosystem - 6 marks; Clearly identifies ONE ecosystem, explains how ONE human induced modification influences the ecosystem - 4-5 marks; Identifies ONE ecosystem, describes ONE human induced modification of the ecosystem - 2-3 marks; Provides basic information on an ecosystem or human modifications - 1 mark. Answers could include: Coral Reefs: climate change - increases water temperatures which decrease coral functioning and leads to coral bleaching. Alpine: tourism - construction of ski industry has disrupted the biodiversity of the area causing permanent modifications with the ecosystem. Fiordland: introduced species - introduced species such as the Tahr goat eat large amounts of the natural vegetation and trample the natural vegetation causing damage."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q21c", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [21], "marks": 10, "directive": "Evaluate", "question": "Select ONE ecosystem from Australia OR ONE ecosystem from outside Australia. Using the selected ecosystem, answer Question 21.\n(c) Evaluate TWO responses and/or strategies that have been used to maintain ecosystem functioning.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (10 marks, band descriptors): Clearly identifies ONE ecosystem, detailed judgment of TWO responses and/or strategies that have been used to maintain ecosystem functioning - 9-10 marks; Clearly identifies ONE ecosystem, clear judgment of TWO responses and/or strategies - 7-8 marks; Clearly identifies ONE ecosystem, some judgment of TWO responses and/or strategies - 5-6 marks; Identifies ONE ecosystem, provides some reasoning of ONE/TWO responses and/or strategies - 3-4 marks; Makes some reference to an ecosystem and/or responses and/or strategies - 1-2 marks. Answers could include (Coral Reef): 1) Zoning - has been a positive strategy in maintaining the function of the coral ecosystem because it ensures that high risk areas have been designated as restricted zones so that humans can't access these areas. The current Zoning Plan, introduced in 2004, is helping secure a better future for the GBR while supporting the vital social and economic benefits gained from Reef related tourism and recreation, commercial activities, and fishing. However, while Zoning has been effective in managing human use of the GBR it is ineffective against problems such as climate change and coral bleaching as the zones can't restrict the spread of warmer water throughout the GBR. 2) The Reef 2050 Water Quality Improvement Plan - was developed in 2017 to provide long-term management of water quality across the reef ecosystem. It covers not just the water on the reef itself but also importantly deals with the land that makes up the river catchments that eventually flow out onto the reef. The aim of the plan is to reduce 80% of dissolved nitrogen and 50% of sediment in the waters going into the reef from land-based catchments. This improvement plan has had moderate success with a small improvement across the whole area in water quality however there has been a much higher success with the reduction in pesticides across the area showing that the plan has achieved some levels of success."}, {"id": "indep2025_q20a", "source": "Independent Trial 2025", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [17], "marks": 3, "directive": "How", "question": "Refer to your knowledge of ecosystems and global biodiversity to answer Questions 20(a)-(b). How do Indigenous Peoples contribute to the contemporary management of ecosystems?", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: Explains the role of Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems (3 marks); Describes the role of Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems (2 marks); Identifies/outlines the role of Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems (1 mark). Sample answer: Indigenous Peoples in various regions of the world have maintained a relationship of stewardship with ecosystems, whereby they act as custodians of the land, responsible for protecting and ensuring sustainability for generations to come. Increasingly, governments and the organisations that manage ecosystems have acknowledged the importance of indigenous knowledge and adopted indigenous land management approaches, directly involving Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems. This can be seen in the management of the Great Barrier Reef (GBR) where Traditional Use of Marine Resources Agreements (TUMRA) and Indigenous Land and Sea Ranger Programs have been implemented. These strategies in the GBR draw upon the deep knowledge of First Nations groups within the marine park and directly involve these groups in the responses and strategies used for maintaining ecosystem functioning, biodiversity and sustainability, illustrating the role that Indigenous Peoples have in contemporary ecosystem management."}, {"id": "indep2025_q20b", "source": "Independent Trial 2025", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [15], "marks": 4, "directive": "Discuss", "question": "\"Over time, each generation may come to accept a more degraded environment as 'normal'.\" Discuss this statement with reference to the impact of shifting baselines on the global state of ecosystems.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: Provides a discussion of the impact of shifting baselines on the global state of ecosystems; makes explicit reference to the statement (4 marks); Describes the impact of shifting baselines on the global state of ecosystems; makes implied reference to the statement (3 marks); Outlines the concept of shifting baselines in relation to the global state of ecosystems (2 marks); Makes a series of general statements about shifting baselines or the global state of ecosystems (1 mark). Sample answer: Baseline data refers to the initial set of information which acts as a point of reference for which subsequent data can be compared to, allowing for observations to be made on progress, effectiveness of strategies or the impact of changes. This data is extremely important for researchers studying the global state of ecosystems, in monitoring the health of the natural world and the impact of management on restoring biodiversity and ecological stability. The concept of shifting baselines, however, presents a significant problem to the global state of ecosystems and their future. Shifting baselines refers to the phenomenon in which society's perceptions of what is considered as 'normal' or 'healthy' in the environment changes over time - what may have been considered as degradation in an ecosystem in the past, may now be looked at as normal and accepted. This process, also referred to as Shifting Baseline Syndrome, is detrimental to the global state of ecosystems and their future sustainability, as it changes what is acceptable in regard to conservation. Additionally, it significantly impacts ecosystem management, as responses and strategies can be less effective due to baseline data being too conservative, and minimising society's efforts to address issues in the natural world."}, {"id": "indep2025_q22a", "source": "Independent Trial 2025", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [18], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe the spatial patterns and nature of biodiversity in ONE ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: Provides a clear description of the spatial pattern and nature of biodiversity in an ecosystem studied; demonstrates an extensive understanding of the ecosystem studied (4 marks); Describes the spatial pattern and/or nature of biodiversity in an ecosystem studied; demonstrates sound understanding of the ecosystem studied (3 marks); Identifies and outlines spatial patterns and/or characteristics of biodiversity in an ecosystem studied (2 marks); Makes a series of general statements about an ecosystem studied (1 mark). Sample answer: The Great Barrier Reef (GBR) is an aquatic ecosystem located off the east coast of Queensland, Australia, which covers an area of 344 400 square kilometres and spans 2300 kilometres north to south between 8-24S of the equator in the Tropic of Capricorn. The GBR is made up of over 2000 individual reefs, along with other physical features such as coral cays and islands. Additionally, it is one of the most biodiverse ecosystems on the planet, with thousands of individual species, including over 1600 species of fish and approximately 600 species of corals. Coral requires very specific conditions to survive and are a keystone species in the GBR, acting as the primary producers for energy in the system and providing a physical home to the immense number of marine species inhabiting the reef ecosystem. Changes in the environmental conditions of the GBR, particularly climate change, can have a significant impact on corals and greatly influence the dynamic equilibrium of the entire ecosystem."}, {"id": "indep2025_q22c", "source": "Independent Trial 2025", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [21], "marks": 10, "directive": "Analyse", "question": "Analyse the responses and strategies used for maintaining ecosystem functioning in ONE ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: Provides a detailed analysis of responses and strategies used for maintaining ecosystem functioning in an ecosystem studied; refers to relevant illustrative examples from an ecosystem studied; presents a logical and cohesive response using appropriate geographical terms and concepts (9-10 marks); Analyses responses and strategies used for maintaining ecosystem functioning; refers to illustrative examples; presents a structured response (7-8 marks); Outlines responses and strategies used for maintaining ecosystem functioning; may refer to illustrative examples (5-6 marks); Identifies responses and strategies used in ecosystems; refers to some geographical terms and/or concepts (3-4 marks); Makes a series of general statements about an ecosystem studied (1-2 marks). Sample answer: Australia's Great Barrier Reef (GBR) faces considerable threats from both natural and human-induced impacts and, thus, requires an extensive plethora of responses and strategies to maintain its functioning and sustainability. The GBR is the world's largest coral reef ecosystem; located off the Queensland coast in the Coral Sea, it stretches 2300 kilometres from 8-24S and covers an area of 344 400 square kilometres. As one of the most biodiverse ecosystems on the planet its protection for future generations is of the utmost importance. The key threats to the GBR include anthropogenic climate change and the associated coral bleaching, ocean acidification, increasing storm activity and rising sea levels, along with various other human activities (commercial fishing, shipping, mining, agriculture, pollution, etc.) and ecological disturbances such as the Crown-of-Thorns-Starfish (COTS) and tropical cyclones. The GBR became a World Heritage site in 1981 and is therefore primarily managed by the Commonwealth (Federal) Government, with the GBR Marine Park Authority (GBRMPA) acting as the main advisory body, however local governments and the Queensland State Government also play roles in the care and protection of the ecosystem. In recent years, the need to protect the GBR has been made abundantly clear, with reports conducted by the World Wildlife Fund (WWF) stating that the ecosystem may be set for total collapse, based on current projections, by 2050 - highlighting the need for effective responses and strategies that consider both intergenerational and intragenerational equity. The first example of this is zoning - introduced in 2004 by the GBRMPA, zoning maps outline areas where specific activities, such as commercial fishing, tourism developments and First Nations' use of marine resources, can occur. Significant penalties are given to those that do not abide by the zoning maps, which are made available freely online, and this strategy ensures that both current and future generations can interact with, and benefit from, the reef sustainably. Additionally, strict regulations and policies have been placed on human activities from tourism, to mining, agriculture, shipping and fishing. This includes education of tourists and the Environmental Management Charge ('Reef Tax') paid in all tourist activities, funding marine park management; large vessels requiring a 'sea pilot' to alleviate the risk of ships striking the reef; and, the Reef 2050 Water Quality Improvement Plan, aimed at addressing issues with pollutants from agriculture along the coast. A growing emphasis has also been placed on the integration of First Nations' knowledge and methods within contemporary management, which has led to the introduction of Traditional Use of Marine Resources Agreements (TUMRA), the Land and Sea Ranger Program, and the Indigenous Reef Advisory Committee (IRAC) - all of which lean on the extensive knowledge and history of First Nations people in and around the reef to improve overall management. Finally, scientific research and innovation, such as exploring the potential of super corals, artificial insemination of corals, or COTS extermination programs, are continuing to expand the repertoire of responses to changes in the functioning and sustainability of the GBR - combined with the many strategies above, the reef may yet have a future beyond 2050, and one that allows for current generations to witness its wonder and beauty, without the loss of this opportunity for the generations to come."}, {"id": "trial2026-w1", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [18, 19], "marks": 2, "directive": "Identify", "question": "Identify ONE example of primary data and ONE example of secondary information that a geography student could collect when investigating the ecological health of an ecosystem.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 2 marks \u2013 Identifies ONE example of primary data and ONE example of secondary information that a geography student could collect when investigating the ecological health of an ecosystem. 1 mark \u2013 Identifies ONE relevant example of primary data OR secondary information.\n\nSample answer: Primary data: % of kelp measured by quadrat across a transect. Secondary information: Satellite photography of a kelp forest showing change over time."}, {"id": "trial2026-w2", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [15, 18], "marks": 2, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Outline ONE way a geography student might use remote sensing in an ecosystem investigation.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 2 marks \u2013 Outlines ONE way a geography student could use remote sensing in an ecosystem investigation. 1 mark \u2013 Provides some relevant information.\n\nSample answer: One way a student could use remote sensing is by analysing satellite imagery of an area over time to track changes in vegetation or canopy cover. This allows the student to identify areas of degradation, regrowth or clearing."}, {"id": "trial2026-w3", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [13], "marks": 2, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Outline ONE resource that ecosystems provide for humans.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 2 marks \u2013 Outlines ONE resource that ecosystems provide for humans. 1 mark \u2013 Identifies a resource that ecosystems provide for humans.\n\nSample answer: Ecosystems provide provisioning resources such as freshwater. Forested catchments and wetlands filter and store water, ensuring a reliable supply for drinking, agriculture and industry. They also help maintain water quality by removing pollutants and reducing sediment runoff, supporting human wellbeing."}, {"id": "trial2026-w4", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [12, 18], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe the role of ONE plant or animal species that is important to the functioning of an ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 4 marks \u2013 Describes the role of ONE plant or animal species that is important to the functioning of an ecosystem. 3 marks \u2013 Outlines the role of ONE plant or animal species that is important to the functioning of an ecosystem. 2 marks \u2013 Outlines one or more aspects of ONE plant or animal species. 1 mark \u2013 Provides some relevant information about plants or animals.\n\nSample answer: Dipterocarp trees are a keystone species in the Borneo rainforest, forming the emergent canopy and providing food, shelter and breeding habitats for a wide range of animals, including orangutans, hornbills and insects. Their irregular mass fruiting events influence population dynamics across the ecosystem by supplying large quantities of food. Extensive root systems help stabilise soils and support nutrient cycling in nutrient-poor conditions. As a result, the loss of dipterocarps through logging can reduce biodiversity, disrupt ecosystem processes and weaken overall ecosystem functioning."}, {"id": "trial2026-w5", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [17], "marks": 5, "directive": "Discuss", "question": "Discuss the role played by Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 5 marks \u2013 Discusses clearly and thoroughly the role played by Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems. 4 marks \u2013 Provides some discussion of the role played by Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems. 3 marks \u2013 Describes the role played by Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems. 2 marks \u2013 Outlines ONE role played by Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems. 1 mark \u2013 Provides some relevant information.\n\nSample answer: Indigenous Peoples contribute significantly to ecosystem management through Traditional Ecological Knowledge (TEK), developed through thousands of years of environmental stewardship. In Australia, Indigenous Ranger programs combine TEK with Western scientific methods to manage fire regimes, control invasive species and restore degraded environments. The regeneration of the Jewell Wetlands demonstrates how Indigenous knowledge and controlled ecological burns can support ecosystem recovery. However, the success of these approaches depends on genuine collaboration with Indigenous communities rather than tokenistic consultation. Overall, Indigenous involvement improves ecological resilience, supports cultural continuity and strengthens the long-term sustainability of ecosystem management."}, {"id": "trial2026-w12", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [16], "marks": 6, "directive": "Evaluate", "question": "Evaluate ONE successful ecosystem conservation program for its sustainability.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 6 marks \u2013 Evaluates with criteria ONE successful ecosystem conservation program for its sustainability. 4\u20135 marks \u2013 Assesses ONE successful ecosystem conservation program for its sustainability. 3 marks \u2013 Describes ONE ecosystem conservation program. 2 marks \u2013 Outlines ONE ecosystem conservation program. 1 mark \u2013 Makes general statements related to ecosystem conservation.\n\nSample answer: The tiger conservation program in Thailand\u2019s Eastern and Western Forest Complexes is a successful ecosystem conservation program when evaluated against ecological effectiveness, community engagement and long-term viability. Ecologically, it has been highly effective, with trail cameras, SMART patrolling and surveillance technology reducing poaching and supporting tiger population recovery, evidenced by the return of tigers and the observation of new cubs. The program has also engaged local communities by supporting livelihoods and involving residents in conservation activities, increasing long-term support for protection efforts. Its sustainability is further strengthened through ongoing monitoring, international cooperation and education campaigns that address illegal wildlife trade. Overall, the program demonstrates strong sustainability because it combines technology, enforcement and community participation. However, its long-term success remains dependent on continued funding and effective control of poaching and illegal wildlife markets."}, {"id": "trial2026-w13", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [18], "marks": 2, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Understanding ecosystem spatial patterns, the value of natural places and approaches to research is important when managing ecosystems.\n\n(a) Outline the spatial pattern of ONE ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 2 marks \u2013 Outlines the spatial pattern of ONE ecosystem. 1 mark \u2013 Provides some relevant information.\n\nSample answer: The Great Southern Reef is a temperate kelp forest ecosystem extending approximately 8,000 km along the continental shelf of southern Australia, from northern New South Wales to Kalbarri in Western Australia. It covers around 71,000 km\u00b2 at depths of 30\u201360 metres in the sub-tidal zone, dominated by Golden Kelp (Ecklonia radiata)."}, {"id": "trial2026-w14", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [13], "marks": 6, "directive": "Analyse", "question": "Understanding ecosystem spatial patterns, the value of natural places and approaches to research is important when managing ecosystems.\n\n(b) Analyse the value of the ecosystem you outlined in part (a) (i.e. ONE ecosystem you have studied).", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 6 marks \u2013 Identifies components of value in an ecosystem and draws out and relates implications for each component thoroughly. 5 marks \u2013 Identifies components of value in an ecosystem and draws out and relates some implications. 3\u20134 marks \u2013 Describes the value of an ecosystem. 2 marks \u2013 Outlines the value of an ecosystem. 1 mark \u2013 Provides some relevant information.\n\nSample answer: The Great Southern Reef provides important supporting, provisioning, regulating and cultural values. As a supporting service, kelp forests drive primary productivity and nutrient cycling, forming the foundation of marine food webs. This enables provisioning services, as valuable fisheries such as abalone, rock lobster and finfish depend on healthy kelp habitats for their survival. The reef also delivers regulating value by storing carbon, reducing wave energy and improving water quality, helping protect coastal environments. Culturally, it supports tourism, recreation and Indigenous connections to Country, contributing to social wellbeing and local economies. These values are closely linked: if kelp forests decline, fish stocks, coastal protection and recreational opportunities are also reduced. Therefore, protecting the reef\u2019s supporting functions is essential to maintaining its broader ecological, economic and cultural value."}, {"id": "trial2026-w15", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [23], "marks": 12, "directive": "Assess", "question": "Understanding ecosystem spatial patterns, the value of natural places and approaches to research is important when managing ecosystems.\n\n(c) Assess the role of contemporary research and innovation in the sustainable management of the ecosystem you outlined in part (a) (i.e. ONE ecosystem you have studied).", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (extended-response marking bands):\n11\u201312 marks \u2013 Demonstrates comprehensive knowledge and understanding of the role of contemporary research and innovation in the sustainable management of an ecosystem; makes extensive judgement about the value, quality, outcomes, results or size of the role; integrates relevant case studies, illustrative examples and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate; presents a logical and cohesive response using appropriate geographical information, terms and concepts.\n8\u201310 marks \u2013 Demonstrates well-developed knowledge and understanding of the role of contemporary research and innovation in the sustainable management of an ecosystem; makes some judgement about the value, quality, outcomes, results or size of the role; refers to relevant case studies, illustrative examples and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate; presents a logical response using appropriate geographical information and terms.\n5\u20137 marks \u2013 Demonstrates a sound understanding of the role of contemporary research and innovation in the sustainable management of an ecosystem; describes contemporary research and innovation; refers to case studies, illustrative examples and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate; presents a structured response using appropriate geographical information.\n3\u20134 marks \u2013 Demonstrates some basic knowledge and understanding of contemporary research and innovation in the sustainable management of an ecosystem; may refer to case studies, illustrative examples and/or the Stimulus Booklet; uses some geographical information.\n1\u20132 marks \u2013 Demonstrates elementary understanding of contemporary research and innovation in sustainable management.\n\nAnswers could include (Great Southern Reef kelp forest example):\n- Active kelp restoration (Queenscliff Marine Science Centre): Over six million Golden Kelp spores cultivated on twine and gravel for deployment in Port Phillip Bay, an area degraded by purple urchin overgrazing (\u201curchin barrens\u201d); represents a shift from passive protection to active, research-driven habitat rebuilding.\n- Genetic resilience (Golden Kelp seed bank): Victoria\u2019s first Golden Kelp seed bank preserves genetic diversity sourced from multiple locations across Port Phillip Bay; safeguards the genetic resilience needed for kelp to adapt to future environmental stress as climate change continues to warm GSR waters.\n- Green Gravel technique: Juvenile kelp seeded onto small gravel stones under controlled conditions, then deployed at scale into degraded reef areas; scalable and community-accessible, allowing broader participation in restoration; enables scientists to select thermally tolerant genotypes, \u201cfuture-proofing\u201d reefs against further warming and addressing the root cause of kelp decline, not just its symptoms.\n- Marine Protected Areas (MPAs): Supported by ongoing research and monitoring, MPAs provide a complementary management layer by limiting harmful activities such as overfishing and habitat destruction; effectiveness depends on adequate enforcement and community compliance.\n- Judgement may include: Research and innovation have been highly effective in advancing GSR management, moving conservation beyond protection alone towards active, science-led restoration that targets the specific mechanisms of kelp decline \u2014 overgrazing, genetic vulnerability and thermal stress. However, these innovations remain localised and resource-intensive; techniques such as Green Gravel and spore cultivation currently operate at a small scale relative to the reef\u2019s 71,000 km\u00b2 extent. Research and innovation are indispensable to the GSR\u2019s future but cannot alone offset climate change impacts \u2014 they must be paired with broader emissions reduction and MPA enforcement to achieve reef-wide resilience."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-1", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 6, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain how biophysical interactions contribute to the functioning of an ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5-6 marks \u2013 explains multiple, named biophysical interactions (e.g. energy flows, nutrient cycling, food webs, climate\u2013vegetation\u2013soil relationships) using specific detail from a studied ecosystem, and links these clearly to how the ecosystem functions.\n3-4 marks \u2013 describes biophysical interactions with some specific detail but limited explanation of functioning.\n1-2 marks \u2013 general statements about ecosystems with little specific detail.\n\nSample approach: name the ecosystem, then explain at least two linked interactions \u2014 e.g. in a coral reef, symbiosis between coral polyps and zooxanthellae drives primary productivity, which supports a food web from herbivorous fish to apex predators, while nutrient cycling (e.g. via parrotfish grazing and bioerosion) recycles nutrients that would otherwise be scarce in nutrient-poor tropical waters."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-2", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Using an example, describe how nutrient cycling affects the functioning of an ecosystem.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks \u2013 describes nutrient cycling with specific, named detail (e.g. a named nutrient pathway, storage, and named ecosystem) and links it to ecosystem functioning.\n2-3 marks \u2013 describes nutrient cycling in general terms or with a limited example.\n1 mark \u2013 identifies that nutrient cycling occurs, with little detail.\n\nSample approach: e.g. in a mangrove ecosystem, leaf litter decomposes and returns nitrogen and phosphorus to sediments, which are taken up by mangrove roots and by algae that support fish nurseries \u2014 disruption to this cycle (e.g. clearing) reduces productivity throughout the food web."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-3", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 6, "directive": "Discuss", "question": "Discuss how ONE ecosystem you have studied is both vulnerable and resilient to natural and human-induced change.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5-6 marks \u2013 discusses both vulnerability (susceptibility to damage) AND resilience (capacity to recover) with specific, named evidence for the studied ecosystem.\n3-4 marks \u2013 discusses one of vulnerability or resilience with specific detail, or both in general terms.\n1-2 marks \u2013 general statements only.\n\nSample approach: e.g. the Great Barrier Reef is vulnerable to ocean warming (coral bleaching events) and crown-of-thorns starfish outbreaks, yet shows resilience through coral recruitment after disturbance and genetic diversity across the reef system that allows some coral populations to recover faster than others."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-4", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 2, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Outline how ONE example of human-induced stress has affected the functioning of an ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n2 marks \u2013 outlines a named human-induced stress and its effect on functioning.\n1 mark \u2013 identifies a stress with limited or no explanation of effect.\n\nSample approach: e.g. urban stormwater runoff into a wetland introduces excess nutrients, triggering algal blooms that reduce oxygen levels and disrupt the wetland's food web."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-5", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 4, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain how the removal of ONE species from a food web could affect the diversity and functioning of an ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks \u2013 explains how removing a named species disrupts named trophic relationships, with a clear consequence for diversity and functioning.\n2-3 marks \u2013 describes an effect with some specific detail.\n1 mark \u2013 general statement only.\n\nSample approach: e.g. removing sea otters (a keystone predator) from a kelp forest allows sea urchin populations to grow unchecked, leading to overgrazing of kelp and the formation of urchin barrens, which collapses habitat for many other species and reduces biodiversity."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-6", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 2, "directive": "Identify", "question": "Identify what is meant by 'genetic diversity', and outline why it is a value used to justify protecting ecosystems.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n2 marks \u2013 correctly identifies genetic diversity (variation within a species) AND outlines its value for protection (e.g. resilience to disease/climate change, future scientific/medical use).\n1 mark \u2013 addresses only the definition or only the value.\n\nSample approach: genetic diversity is the variety of genes within a species' population. It is valued because populations with greater genetic diversity are more likely to include individuals able to survive new threats (disease, changing conditions), helping the species and ecosystem persist."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-7", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 6, "directive": "Evaluate", "question": "Evaluate the importance of ecosystem management and protection, using at least TWO values (e.g. utility, intrinsic, heritage, or genetic diversity value).", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5-6 marks \u2013 evaluates at least two named values with specific supporting detail and reaches a judgement about their relative or combined importance.\n3-4 marks \u2013 explains two values with some specific detail but limited judgement.\n1-2 marks \u2013 identifies values with little explanation.\n\nSample approach: utility value (e.g. a reef's role in coastal protection and fisheries) can be weighed against intrinsic value (a species' right to exist regardless of human use) \u2014 argue that while utility value is easier to quantify and often drives funding decisions, intrinsic and heritage values are essential for protecting ecosystems that have no obvious economic use but are ecologically or culturally significant."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-8", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe how ONE traditional and ONE contemporary management strategy have each been used in an ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks \u2013 describes one named traditional strategy AND one named contemporary strategy with specific detail.\n2-3 marks \u2013 describes one strategy in detail, or both in general terms.\n1 mark \u2013 identifies a strategy with little detail.\n\nSample approach: traditional \u2014 Indigenous cultural burning practices used to manage fuel loads and promote biodiversity in a savanna ecosystem; contemporary \u2014 satellite-monitored Marine Protected Area zoning that restricts fishing in a reef ecosystem based on real-time data."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-9", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 8, "directive": "Evaluate", "question": "Evaluate the effectiveness of ONE traditional and ONE contemporary management strategy used in an ecosystem at risk you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n7-8 marks \u2013 evaluates both strategies against explicit criteria (e.g. scale, cost, measurable outcomes, community involvement), using specific named evidence, and reaches a substantiated overall judgement.\n5-6 marks \u2013 describes both strategies with some evaluation but a less sustained judgement.\n3-4 marks \u2013 describes strategies with limited evaluation.\n1-2 marks \u2013 general statements only.\n\nSample approach: compare a traditional practice (e.g. cultural burning) against a contemporary strategy (e.g. a Marine Protected Area or reef-restoration project) using measurable outcomes (area protected, species recovery data, cost, community engagement), concluding which is more effective and why, while acknowledging limitations of each."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-10", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe the spatial patterns and dimensions (location, altitude, latitude, size, shape and continuity) of an ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks \u2013 describes at least three of the named dimensions with specific detail for the studied ecosystem.\n2-3 marks \u2013 describes one or two dimensions with specific detail.\n1 mark \u2013 general statement about the ecosystem's location only.\n\nSample answer: e.g. the Great Southern Reef stretches along Australia's temperate southern coastline (latitude range roughly 27\u00b0S to 43\u00b0S), extending from shallow subtidal zones down to around 40m depth (altitude/depth dimension), is continuous along thousands of kilometres of coast but varies in width and continuity depending on suitable rocky substrate for kelp attachment."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-11", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe TWO negative human impacts on the functioning of an ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks \u2013 describes two distinct, named human impacts with specific detail and a clear link to ecosystem functioning.\n2-3 marks \u2013 describes one impact in detail, or two in general terms.\n1 mark \u2013 identifies an impact with little detail.\n\nSample approach: e.g. sewage-outfall discharge historically increased nutrient loads and reduced water clarity, smothering kelp with algal growth; and coastal development has removed nearshore vegetation buffers, increasing sediment runoff that further reduces light available for kelp photosynthesis."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-12", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 4, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain how ONE natural change and ONE human-induced change have each affected an ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks \u2013 explains one named natural change AND one named human-induced change, each with a clear effect on the ecosystem.\n2-3 marks \u2013 explains one change in detail, or both in general terms.\n1 mark \u2013 identifies a change with little explanation.\n\nSample approach: natural \u2014 a marine heatwave causing coral bleaching and short-term loss of live coral cover; human-induced \u2014 coastal armouring (seawalls) altering natural sediment movement and removing intertidal habitat over time."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-13", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 20, "directive": "Evaluate", "question": "Evaluate the effectiveness of traditional and contemporary management strategies used to protect TWO ecosystems at risk that you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Band 5 (17-20 marks): sustained, well-structured evaluation of traditional AND contemporary strategies across TWO ecosystems, integrating specific named case-study evidence (statistics, project names, outcomes) throughout, and reaching a well-substantiated overall judgement with explicit criteria.\nBand 4 (13-16 marks): sound evaluation of strategies for two ecosystems with good specific detail, though integration or judgement may be less consistent.\nBand 3 (9-12 marks): describes strategies for one or two ecosystems with some evaluation, but relies more on description than judgement.\nBand 2 (5-8 marks): general description of ecosystem management with limited specific detail or case-study evidence.\nBand 1 (1-4 marks): isolated or irrelevant statements.\n\nSample approach: structure around two contrasting ecosystems (e.g. a reef ecosystem and a wetland or rainforest ecosystem), for each naming one traditional and one contemporary strategy, using statistics/outcomes to judge effectiveness, and concluding with a comparative judgement about which combinations of strategies proved most effective and why."}];